# EventFlow
ID	Document	MoSCoW	Purpose	Must Answer	Summary	a	Status	Dependency	Code Blocked?	Notes
EV-001	README.md	MUST	Project entry point; explain EventFlow, core workflow, setup and links.	What is EventFlow and how do I run it?	Project entry point: what EventFlow is, the six-stage workflow, repository layout, how to run the static UI prototype, the canonical units and terminology, and current build status.	P5	Review		Yes	
EV-002	PRD.md	MUST	Define product problem, users, scope, MVP features and behavior.	What are we building and what is in/out of scope?		P5	Not Started		Yes	
EV-003	Architecture.md	MUST	Define architecture, folder structure, component boundaries and responsibility.	Where does every component live and what does it own?		P3	Not Started		Yes	
EV-004	Domain_Model.md	MUST	Define EventFlow entities and terminology.	What does every core concept mean?		P5	Not Started		Yes	
EV-005	Data_Model.md	MUST	Define PostgreSQL tables, fields, relationships and constraints.	How is data stored and related?		P3	Not Started		Yes	
EV-006	Graph_model.md	MUST	Define graph nodes, edges, capacity, movement and state.	How is the event ecosystem represented as a graph?	Full node and directed-edge schemas with entity-scoped units (node capacity in people, edge capacity in people/minute), traversal and closure rules, Initial vs Current Graph, single-write-path status provenance, validation and simulation isolation.	P1	Review		Yes	
EV-007	Crowd_model.md	MUST	Define crowd movement, accumulation, utilization and overload calculations.	How is crowd state calculated?	Aggregate crowd groups with a five-state machine, movement and flow equations, conservation with separately recorded adjustments, utilization, density, overload, queue and waiting/travel time, routing execution and a complete metric contract.	P1	Review		Yes	
EV-008	Disruption_model.md	MUST	Define how rain, closures and transport disruption modify the network.	How does a disruption change the network?	Canonical disruption schema across nine types and four severity levels that are never converted into physical quantities, a DETECTED/ACTIVE/RESOLVED lifecycle, declared operational effects, timing, conflict rules and simulation isolation.	P1	Review		Yes	
EV-009	Prediction.md	MUST	Define bottleneck prediction behavior and horizon.	How does EventFlow predict future overcrowding?	Forecasting contract with a configurable horizon, exact input and output schemas, confidence defined as reliability in [0,100], staleness and refresh rules, a deterministic fallback, mandatory explanations and a NO_PREDICTION failure path.	P1	Review		Yes	
EV-010	Optimization.md	MUST	Define OR-Tools variables, constraints, objectives and outputs.	What mathematical problem does optimization solve?	Six bounded decision variables, nine hard constraints, a weighted and normalized objective, an OR-Tools mapping, deterministic ranking with stable tie-breaks, six result statuses and the operator approval handoff.	P1	Review		Yes	
EV-011	Simulation.md	MUST	Define sandbox/digital-twin simulation and comparison process.	How are strategies tested before action?	Sandbox digital twin with a scenario schema, copied baselines, isolated state, deterministic time, disruption and intervention execution, optional prediction invocation, 17 metrics with units and six result statuses.	P1	Review		Yes	
